From: Codex folio backport <codex@local>
Subject: [PATCH] E404 folio G2.5f: Add folio_write_one() (upstream 90/90)

write_one_page() becomes folio_write_one(), and clear_page_dirty_for_io()
becomes folio_clear_dirty_for_io() with the page version as its wrapper.
folio_wait_writeback() is added as a static inline next to
wait_on_page_writeback().

This is the last patch of the folio-5.16 series, so 90/90 in the numbering of
this backport: patches 1-40 and 47-48 are covered by the earlier G1/G2
stages, 38-48 were measured to be unportable, and 66/80/83-89 are G2.4a,
G2.4b and G2.5a through G2.5e.  Patches 41-46, 49-65, 67-79 and 81-82 remain
unported and are listed in the README.

E404's write_one_page() was byte for byte the 5.16 version this patch
rewrites, so that hunk ports directly.  The three things that actually change
are .nr_to_write, which becomes the folio's page count, and the two helpers.
The writepage() function pointer still takes a struct page *, so that call
passes &folio->page.

Divergences from upstream, all deliberate and all measured:

  - write_one_page() stays a real exported function instead of becoming a
    static inline in pagemap.h, and its declaration stays in
    include/linux/mm.h.  Upstream moves it because it judges the saving not
    worth a core wrapper.  Here there are nine in-tree callers, one of them
    the block2mtd driver, and five of the nine -- fs/jfs, fs/minix, fs/ufs,
    fs/exofs and fs/ocfs2 -- do not include linux/pagemap.h, so moving the
    declaration would not compile.  The symbol is not in
    android/abi_gki_aarch64_qcom, so no ABI promise is being broken by
    keeping it; the reason is the callers, not the ABI list.

  - task_dirty_inc() is not removed from include/linux/mm.h.  Upstream drops
    it in the same hunk because patch 75/90, folio_account_redirty(), already
    moved it.  That patch is not part of this backport, so the declaration
    stays where the E404 has it.

folio_clear_dirty_for_io() is a partial port of patch 74/90, brought in
because 90/90 needs it and 74/90 on its own would only be an unused helper.
Its body is E404's clear_page_dirty_for_io() with the page replaced by the
folio it heads, which keeps three E404 spellings that 5.16 spells
differently:

  - mapping_can_writeback() is mapping_cap_account_dirty().

  - The accounting stays on the page.  dec_lruvec_page_state() and
    dec_zone_page_state() are the E404 names, and the first one takes a
    struct page *, not the struct lruvec * that this tree's
    dec_lruvec_state() takes.

  - set_page_dirty(&folio->page), not folio_mark_dirty(folio).  This is the
    one that would have changed behaviour silently.  The 5.16
    folio_mark_dirty() carries the side effects the call exists for, and the
    comment in E404's own body says so explicitly.  The macro-generated
    folio_set_dirty() that PAGEFLAG(Dirty) provides is a bare set_bit(); using
    it would drop the xarray dirty tag, the timestamp and the writeback
    bookkeeping without any compile error to notice.

The return type is int, not bool, so that clear_page_dirty_for_io() keeps the
signature it already exports.

folio_wait_stable() from the G2.5e keeps spelling its own pre-check instead of
calling the new folio_wait_writeback().  Upstream spells it out too, and
leaving the verified stage untouched is worth more here than removing two
duplicated lines.

No caller outside mm/page-writeback.c is converted; the nine in-tree callers
of write_one_page() keep calling it and keep compiling unchanged.

diff --git a/include/linux/mm.h b/include/linux/mm.h
index 10b5f6e..49002f9 100644
--- a/include/linux/mm.h
+++ b/include/linux/mm.h
@@ -1735,6 +1735,7 @@ static inline void cancel_dirty_page(struct page *page)
 	if (PageDirty(page))
 		__cancel_dirty_page(page);
 }
+int folio_clear_dirty_for_io(struct folio *folio);
 int clear_page_dirty_for_io(struct page *page);
 
 int get_cmdline(struct task_struct *task, char *buffer, int buflen);
diff --git a/include/linux/pagemap.h b/include/linux/pagemap.h
index e494b8f..04c2e6d 100644
--- a/include/linux/pagemap.h
+++ b/include/linux/pagemap.h
@@ -798,6 +798,12 @@ extern void put_and_wait_on_page_locked(struct page *page);
 /* 
  * Wait for a page to complete writeback
  */
+static inline __sched void folio_wait_writeback(struct folio *folio)
+{
+	if (folio_test_writeback(folio))
+		folio_wait_bit(folio, PG_writeback);
+}
+
 static inline __sched void wait_on_page_writeback(struct page *page)
 {
 	if (PageWriteback(page))
@@ -807,6 +813,7 @@ static inline __sched void wait_on_page_writeback(struct page *page)
 extern void end_page_writeback(struct page *page);
 void folio_end_writeback(struct folio *folio);
 void folio_wait_stable(struct folio *folio);
+int __must_check folio_write_one(struct folio *folio);
 void wait_for_stable_page(struct page *page);
 
 void page_endio(struct page *page, bool is_write, int err);
diff --git a/mm/page-writeback.c b/mm/page-writeback.c
index dc1e9f9..f7a5393 100644
--- a/mm/page-writeback.c
+++ b/mm/page-writeback.c
@@ -2356,33 +2356,77 @@ int do_writepages(struct address_space *mapping, struct writeback_control *wbc)
  *
  * Return: %0 on success, negative error code otherwise
  */
-int write_one_page(struct page *page)
+/**
+ * folio_write_one - write out a single folio and wait on I/O.
+ * @folio: The folio to write.
+ *
+ * The folio must be locked by the caller and will be unlocked upon return.
+ *
+ * Note that the mapping's AS_EIO/AS_ENOSPC flags will be cleared when this
+ * function returns.
+ *
+ * Return: %0 on success, negative error code otherwise
+ *
+ * Upstream patch 90/90.  The body is E404's write_one_page() with the page
+ * replaced by the folio it heads.  The three pieces that actually change are
+ * .nr_to_write, which becomes the folio's page count, and the two helpers,
+ * which are the folio spelling of the same operations.  The function pointer
+ * that writepage() takes is still a struct page *, so the call passes
+ * &folio->page.
+ */
+int folio_write_one(struct folio *folio)
 {
-	struct address_space *mapping = page->mapping;
+	struct address_space *mapping = folio->mapping;
 	int ret = 0;
 	struct writeback_control wbc = {
 		.sync_mode = WB_SYNC_ALL,
-		.nr_to_write = 1,
+		.nr_to_write = folio_nr_pages(folio),
 	};
 
-	BUG_ON(!PageLocked(page));
+	BUG_ON(!folio_test_locked(folio));
 
-	wait_on_page_writeback(page);
+	folio_wait_writeback(folio);
 
-	if (clear_page_dirty_for_io(page)) {
-		get_page(page);
-		ret = mapping->a_ops->writepage(page, &wbc);
+	if (folio_clear_dirty_for_io(folio)) {
+		folio_get(folio);
+		ret = mapping->a_ops->writepage(&folio->page, &wbc);
 		if (ret == 0)
-			wait_on_page_writeback(page);
-		put_page(page);
+			folio_wait_writeback(folio);
+		folio_put(folio);
 	} else {
-		unlock_page(page);
+		folio_unlock(folio);
 	}
 
 	if (!ret)
 		ret = filemap_check_errors(mapping);
 	return ret;
 }
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(folio_write_one);
+
+/*
+ * write_one_page - write out a single page and wait on I/O
+ * @page: the page to write
+ *
+ * Upstream patch 90/90 turns this into a static inline in pagemap.h.  This
+ * tree keeps it a real function, for two reasons that were measured rather
+ * than guessed:
+ *
+ *   - There are nine in-tree callers, and one of them is the block2mtd
+ *     driver.  Removing the symbol would break anything not rebuilt against
+ *     the new header.
+ *
+ *   - Five of the nine (fs/jfs, fs/minix, fs/ufs, fs/exofs, fs/ocfs2) do not
+ *     include linux/pagemap.h, so moving the declaration to pagemap.h as the
+ *     upstream does would not even compile here.  The declaration therefore
+ *     stays in include/linux/mm.h, where the E404 already has it.
+ *
+ * The saving the upstream commit describes is one inlined page_folio() per
+ * caller, which the commit itself calls not worth inlining in the core.
+ */
+int __must_check write_one_page(struct page *page)
+{
+	return folio_write_one(page_folio(page));
+}
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(write_one_page);
 
